Description: install Windows 10 as a dual-boot from Linux without a USB drive
 linwin installs Windows 10 next to an existing Linux system without a USB
 stick or DVD. It creates the partitions in unallocated disk space (or shrinks
 a Btrfs root filesystem online, LUKS included), copies the Windows installer
 to a temporary partition that replaces the USB drive, boots it once through a
 one-time UEFI entry and installs Windows unattended. Linux then becomes the
 default again and Windows is added to the GRUB, systemd-boot, Limine or
 rEFInd menu. Everything can be undone with one command.
 .
 The Intel RST/VMD storage driver and Wi-Fi/Ethernet drivers are added to the
 installer automatically. A GTK 4 interface (linwin-gui) is included; messages
 are in French.
 .
 This package is in contrib because it downloads non-free software at the
 user's request: the Windows installation image, the Intel RST driver from
 Intel and network drivers from the Microsoft Update Catalog.
